    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d527">

                <pb facs="0817" n="796" xml:id="pg796"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.6111/1–2348: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Embassy in the Soviet Union
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-01-23T14:00:00-05:00">January 23, 1948—2
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>89. We are holding in NY Customs 19 pieces of recently arrived Soviet chancery
                    supplies in addition 40 pieces mentioned Deptel 2050 Dec 22.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d527-f1"/> Please indicate
                    action you wish us to take on these consignments.</p>
                <p>Dept also interested learn whether Fonoff has
                    established new customs quota Emb imports for 1948.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d527-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lovett</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d527-f1" n="1" xml:id="d527-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d527-f2" n="2" xml:id="d527-f2-content">
                <p>Ambassador Walter Bedell Smith replied in telegram 129 from Moscow on January 24,
                    not printed, that in view of continued difficulties, and with the 1948 customs
                    quota still 900,000 rubles, he requested that the 19 pieces continue to be held
                    (701.6111/1–2448).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
